        "findings": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe new step slots cleanly into an established pattern in this codebase:\n\n- **Right unit, no catch-all.** `EnqueueOwnerWelcome` is its own single-responsibility step rather than being bolted onto `AuditOwnerCreated`. That's the correct call \u2014 it's a distinct concern (welcome notification on the `NOTIFY` logger vs. auditing on `AUDIT`), and this pipeline consistently favors one small function per concern (`Normalize\u2026`, `Validate\u2026`, `Derive\u2026`, `Audit\u2026`). Folding it into the audit step would have made that class a catch-all.\n- **Idiomatic mirror of the sibling.** It matches `AuditOwnerCreated` exactly: single public `service(@Val Owner owner)` method, a `private static final Logger` for a dedicated named logger, no repository access, and the same rationale for staying under `transaction` governance. The Javadoc even cross-references `AuditOwnerCreated`, `SaveOwner`, and `RespondWithOwnerCreated` in the same style as the existing docs.\n- **Correct wiring/placement.** Inserted after `audit` (so the id is populated post-`save`) and before the derive/respond steps, with `govern: [ transaction ]` consistent with every neighboring step so the create commits as one boundary. The name follows the verb-plus-entity convention.\n\nNo duplication, nothing overloaded, and a maintainer of this codebase would find it natural."
